    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **28 tests, 0 failures**, including the new `Cp17Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field \u2014 a canonical region derived from the owner's city via the fixed table Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, defaulting to `UNKNOWN`.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema, so it's part of the generated `OwnerDto` and appears in GET responses.\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Localities.java`** (new) \u2014 a small static helper holding the city\u2192region table with a `region(city)` method.\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", ...)` expression that calls `Localities.region(owner.getCity())`, matching how the other derived fields (displayName, membershipTier) are computed.\n\n### One trap worth calling out\nMy first attempt put the derivation in a `default String locality(String city)` method on the mapper interface. MapStruct silently interpreted that signature as an **implicit String\u2192String mapping method** and wired it into *every* String property across the whole mapper \u2014 so `telephone` (and every other string) got passed through `locality(...)` and became `\"UNKNOWN\"`, failing the E.164 persist-time validation with a 500. Moving the logic into a standalone static utility class (not part of the mapper interface) fixed it: the generated impl now maps `telephone` straight through and only `locality` uses the region lookup.",
